# Flow DSL

The Flow DSL is compile-time Elixir syntax for canonical Flow data. Its data
expressions are restricted. An inline Action body is normal Elixir code that
compiles to an Action.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

Inline Steps require `3.0.0-beta.5` or later.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.5"}])
```

## Define Small Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Echo do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_echo"

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Append do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_append"

  @impl true
  def run(%{values: values, item: item}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{values: values ++ [item]}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Increment do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_increment"

  @impl true
  def run(%{count: count}, _context), do: {:ok, %{count: count + 1}}
end
```

## Declare The Graph

The seven component forms are `step`, `choice`, `map`, `reduce`, `iterate`,
`dispatch`, and a Flow-derived `subflow`. A child Flow is written as a `step`
whose target is a Flow module. Dispatch can only appear at the end of a
run-to-completion Flow.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Example do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_language_example"

  flow do
    step "seed", value <- input(:value) do
      {:ok, %{value: value}}
    end

    choice "route" do
      option "fast",
        condition: input(:mode) == :fast,
        action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
        params: %{route: :fast, value: result("seed", :value)}

      otherwise(
        action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
        params: %{route: :standard, value: result("seed", :value)}
      )
    end

    map "mapped",
      collection: input(:items),
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: item(), index: item_index()}

    reduce "collected" do
      collection(result("mapped"))
      initial(%{values: value([])})
      action(FlowLanguage.Append)
      params(%{
        values: accumulator(:values),
        item: item(:value)
      })
    end

    iterate "counter" do
      state(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer()}), initial: %{count: 0})
      action(FlowLanguage.Increment)
      params(%{count: state(:count)})
      update(%{count: body_result(:count)})
      repeat(2)
    end

    output(%{
      route: result("route"),
      values: result("collected", :values),
      counter: result("counter", :state)
    })
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.Example, %{
    value: 10,
    mode: :fast,
    items: [1, 2, 3]
  })

result
```

## Keep Headers And Output As Data

A Flow expression, including each inline binding source, can contain:

- `nil`, Boolean values, numbers, strings, and atoms;
- proper lists;
- maps with supported data keys; and
- Flow references.

The unreleased `Jido.Expr` API adds fixed portable operations to these data
fields. See [Expressions](flow-expressions.md). It is not in the beta.5 install
cell above.

These forms are not Flow expressions:

```text
function calls
anonymous functions
pipes
pattern matches
tuples
keyword lists
struct construction
```

Use `value/1` when you want to mark one supported literal explicitly. Use
`select/2` to append a path to a reference. The Reduce example uses
`value([])` to mark its empty-list accumulator as literal data.

These restrictions do not apply inside an inline Action body. It can call
functions, use pipes, match patterns, and create local anonymous functions.
Those functions are not Flow targets. Flow reference helpers have no runtime
meaning in the body; bind their data in the header first. See
[Steps And Output](flow-steps.livemd#write-an-inline-body) for the binding forms.

An inline body has the owning Flow module's function scope. It can use private
helpers, aliases, available imports, declaration-time module attributes, and
`__MODULE__`. It does not capture runtime variables outside the declaration. Jido
removes its declaration imports inside the body. Spark can also remove an
outer import that conflicts with a DSL name. Use a qualified call such as
`MyApp.Helpers.output(value)`, or import that helper inside the body.

Only `step` has the shipped top-level shorthand. The unreleased nested
`action` form also supports Map, Reduce, Choice options and fallback, and
Iterate. Dispatch uses bound `decision` and direct callback `expander` forms.
All forms lower to ordinary Action fields and host data. See
[Portable Inline Actions](inline-actions.md) for the syntax and public
downstream host API. These forms are not in `3.0.0-beta.5`.

## Use Closed Conditions

The DSL supports these condition forms:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
input(:status) == :ready
input(:count) >= 3
input(:tier) in [:gold, :silver]
input(:enabled) == true and input(:blocked) != true
not (input(:status) == :closed)
```

The canonical operators are `eq`, `neq`, `lt`, `lte`, `gt`, `gte`, `in`,
`all`, `any`, and `not`.

## Keep Output Explicit

Every Flow has exactly one `output` declaration, and it must be the final
declaration. Normal Flow output must be a map. An explicit
`Jido.Action.Output` envelope can carry a non-map result.

Continue with [Steps And Output](flow-steps.livemd) and
[References And Data](flow-references.livemd).
